// The cross-stack parity type (WP-CONFORMANCE-AND-PARITY WP-1).
//
// Must match THIS stack's dds.bench perf-data EXACTLY, or the comparison is not
// apples-to-apples:
//   (dds.gen:define-dds-type perf-data (:extensibility :final)
//     (id :i32 :key t)
//     (data (:sequence :u8)))
//
//   - @final extensibility (our define-dds-type uses :final)
//   - @key on id
//   - data is a sequence<octet> bounded at 65536: ONE type serves the whole payload ladder
//     (32 B .. 64 KB), and the serialized shape (4-byte length prefix + N octets) is
//     identical in both stacks, so neither side is advantaged by the type's framing.
//     WHY AN EXPLICIT BOUND: our Lisp type is UNBOUNDED, but rtiddsgen silently bounds an
//     unbounded sequence at 100 elements (a 256-byte payload then throws
//     "bounded_sequence would go over its MaxLength" at write()). The bound changes the
//     TypeObject, NOT the wire — the serialized payload is length-prefixed either way — so
//     the parity comparison is unaffected. (Same rtiddsgen-bounds-what-you-left-unbounded
//     behaviour that ADR 0009 documents for `string`.)
//   - default @autoid(SEQUENTIAL) -> member ids 0,1 (matches our decl-index ids)

@final
struct PerfData {
    @key long id;
    sequence<octet, 65536> data;
};
